<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Antonine Wall</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">142</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century">2nd century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Antonine Wall was a Roman turf rampart built across the Forth–Clyde isthmus of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> from 142 on the orders of Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antoninus-pius">Antoninus Pius</a>. It marked the northern frontier of Roman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> for about twenty years before the army withdrew to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadrians-wall">Hadrian's Wall</a>. It crossed the land of the Damnonii and is now a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/antonine-wall.jpg" alt="Antonine Wall" width="320" height="399" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Created by NormanEinstein, September 20, 2005; derivative work by Talifero · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hadrians_Wall_map.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Construction began around 142 on the orders of Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antoninus-pius">Antoninus Pius</a>, who never visited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>. Quintus Lollius Urbicus, governor of Roman Britain at the time, first supervised the work. Estimates of how long it took vary widely, with six and twelve years most commonly proposed. Pressure from the Caledonians probably led Antoninus to send the empire's troops farther north. The wall was meant to extend Roman territory and replace <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadrians-wall">Hadrian's Wall</a>, 160 km to the south, as the frontier of Britannia, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> never conquered the Caledonians and the wall suffered many attacks.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The wall ran for about 63 km from Old Kilpatrick on the Firth of Clyde to Carriden, near Bo'ness, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/firth-of-forth">Firth of Forth</a>. It was a bank about 3 metres high and 5 metres wide, of layered turves on a stone foundation, with a wide ditch on the north side and a military road to the south. It is thought a wooden palisade stood on top. Unlike <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadrians-wall">Hadrian's Wall</a> it had no ditch system behind it. The stone foundations and wing walls of the first forts show that a stone wall like Hadrian's was planned at first, but this was quickly changed.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> first planned forts every 10 km, then revised this to every 3.3 km, with at least nine smaller fortlets between them, and a road called the Military Way linked all the sites. Rough Castle Fort is the best preserved, and Kinneil is the most visible fortlet. Coastal forts such as Inveresk served as outposts or supply bases, and forts further north, among them Ardoch and Strageath, were brought back into service. The soldiers who built the wall set up decorated distance slabs naming the length each legion built, and twenty survive. Research at Glasgow University has shown that they were once brightly painted.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The wall was abandoned within two decades of its completion, when the legions withdrew to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadrians-wall">Hadrian's Wall</a> in 162. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/septimius-severus">Septimius Severus</a> campaigned in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> in 208 to 210, there is no evidence that he paid any attention to it. Writing in 730, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bede">Bede</a>, following <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gildas">Gildas</a>, mistakenly ascribed it to the Britons. Medieval chronicles called it Gryme's dyke, which became Graham's dyke, a name still found at Bo'ness.</p><p>William Roy mapped it systematically in 1764, and where later development has destroyed it his drawings are the only reliable record. Sir George Macdonald published his survey in 1911. In the 16th century George Keith, 5th Earl Marischal, set a stone from the wall in the walls of Dunnotar Castle. The Antonine Wall was listed on 7 July 2008 as an extension to the World Heritage Site Frontiers of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a>, and several sites along it are in the care of Historic Environment Scotland.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Antonine Wall” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Hadrian's Wall, begun around 122 and running some 73 miles from the Tyne to the Solway, became the main frontier; the <b>Antonine Wall</b> between the Forth and the Clyde, built from about 142, was held for only some twenty years.</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-britain">Roman Britain · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Their land lay on the line of the <b>Antonine Wall</b>, built across the Forth and Clyde isthmus from 142, and was occupied by Roman forts during the periods of Roman advance into Scotland.</blockquote><a href="/state/damnonii">Damnonii</a></li></ul></section>
